Mostly, the issue with stashing winnable games is
just that the game gets incredibly boring to me past
about DLVL 11 and once my PC is a walking tank.

      This happens awfully early when I can run the
      protection racket reliably at XL:1, to both
      Sokoban and then Mines Town, to arrive at the
      Mines Town altar with _lots_ of gold, and run
      the protection racket for a usual naked AC of
      -1 to -3 or so with all the extra donations
      that staying at XL:1 implies.

      After all, going from XL:1 to XL:2 during the
      protection run is the _worst_ mistake a
      skilled protection racketeer can make.

      Doing so doubles the cost of protection, a
      worse ratio change result than any other
      single XL increase causes, so cuts in half the
      number of donations a certain amount of
      zorkmids can achieve, and does that for a
      trivial amount of extra survivability for a
      practiced pacifist player who accumulates a
      flock of buff pets over the course of the run.

      Nerfing all those excess donations really cuts
      into the chances of getting the extra donation
      good AC boosts past the guaranteed first nine,
      as it takes a _lot_ of donations to beat the
      worsening odds for each extra protection
      boost, and that "lots of donations" works out
      by far the best if the donations are all
      doable at 400z.

      If you set your AC improvement goal at "nine",
      then arriving at the Mines Town altar above
      XL:1 doesn't look so stupid, but if you set
      your AC improvement goal at "as many as
      possible", it sure does.

I don't really have all that many games that need
stashing, though, per games played, because of the
other issues listed. Even a "walking tank" PC
starves exactly as easily if the player carelessly
ignores the status line.

